WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.090
Every animal starts small and grows
up. Some change shape on the way.

2
00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:13.583
A tadpole becomes a frog. A
caterpillar becomes a butterfly.

3
00:00:14.083 --> 00:00:18.402
A frog starts as eggs in jelly
in a pond. This is frogspawn.

4
00:00:18.622 --> 00:00:23.730
A tadpole hatches. It has a tail and
no legs, and breathes in the water.

5
00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:29.012
Back legs grow first, then front
legs. The tail starts to shrink.

6
00:00:29.232 --> 00:00:34.759
Now it is a froglet: legs front and back,
and a stub of tail. It can climb out.

7
00:00:34.979 --> 00:00:40.040
An adult frog has no tail and
breathes air. It lays eggs of its own.

8
00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:45.694
A tadpole is not a baby fish. It is a
baby frog that does not look like one.

9
00:00:46.194 --> 00:00:49.909
A butterfly starts as a
tiny egg, laid on a leaf.

10
00:00:50.129 --> 00:00:55.238
A caterpillar hatches and eats
leaves all day, growing and growing.

11
00:00:55.458 --> 00:01:00.427
Then it hangs itself up and becomes
a chrysalis. It stays very still.

12
00:01:00.647 --> 00:01:05.476
Inside the chrysalis it is changing
completely. It has not died.

13
00:01:05.696 --> 00:01:10.015
A butterfly comes out, dries its
wings, and flies off to lay eggs.

14
00:01:10.515 --> 00:01:14.277
A human baby cannot walk or
talk yet, and it drinks milk.

15
00:01:14.497 --> 00:01:18.259
But it already has the shape of
a person. It only grows bigger.

16
00:01:18.479 --> 00:01:22.612
A child, then an adult, fully
grown. That takes many years.

17
00:01:22.832 --> 00:01:27.383
A chick hatches fluffy. In a few
weeks it has feathers and can fly.

18
00:01:27.603 --> 00:01:32.572
Same shape, bigger. A bird does
not change shape like a frog.

19
00:01:33.072 --> 00:01:36.648
So there are two ways to grow
up. Sort each baby animal.

20
00:01:36.868 --> 00:01:42.766
Keep your shape and get bigger: a puppy,
a chick, a foal, a kitten, a baby.

21
00:01:42.986 --> 00:01:48.651
Or change shape completely: a tadpole,
a caterpillar, a ladybird larva.

22
00:01:48.871 --> 00:01:52.122
Do not go by size. Look
for the change of shape.

23
00:01:52.622 --> 00:01:57.498
Animals take different times to grow
up. A table lets you compare them.

24
00:01:57.718 --> 00:02:00.505
A butterfly: egg to butterfly
in only a few weeks.

25
00:02:00.725 --> 00:02:04.254
A frog: frogspawn to
froglet takes a few months.

26
00:02:04.474 --> 00:02:09.118
A human takes many years. The bigger
the animal, the longer it takes.

27
00:02:09.618 --> 00:02:15.609
Scientists use models. A model shows
an idea clearly, leaving the rest out.

28
00:02:15.829 --> 00:02:21.402
A drawing of the frog's life cycle,
with labels, is a diagram. You read it.

29
00:02:21.622 --> 00:02:25.708
A plastic butterfly with wings
that open is a physical model.

30
00:02:25.928 --> 00:02:31.733
A globe, or a toy skeleton. You can
hold a physical model and turn it.

31
00:02:32.233 --> 00:02:36.041
A frog and a butterfly change
shape completely as they grow.

32
00:02:36.261 --> 00:02:39.651
A chick and a baby keep their
shape and only get bigger.

33
00:02:39.871 --> 00:02:44.376
Every animal grows up at its own
speed: weeks, months or years.

34
00:02:44.596 --> 00:02:49.705
A diagram you read. A physical
model you hold. Both show an idea.
